      "writtenOn": "2012-08-29T17:56:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is the one part of this change that I think requires some deep consideration.  Is it possible to wind up in a state where we fail to restart the runtime, wedging the watchdog itself, because the \u0027sync\u0027 fails to complete?\n\nIn general if the kernel/filesystems are in such a bad state that \u0027sync\u0027 fails, then the watchdog restart of the Android runtime is not likely to work anyway.  If a failure in \u0027sync\u0027 here would basically be a guarantee that the watchdog restart would fail anyway, then I think this is fine.  (In particular, the watchdog has already done filesystem activity by attempting to capture stack traces to the ANR trace file; if the filesystem were dead then we would already have hit the wall.\n\nThis is a longwinded way of my saying that I\u0027ve thought a little about whether this is safe, and decided that it\u0027s equivalent to the existing implementation in terms of risking data loss or rebootability, so the concept is fine.  :)",
      "revId": "65191500f270fd14bfc19f461f74b2bb336d6ffc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
